# Build context for Dockerfile.test ONLY.
#
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` first and uses it
# instead of `.dockerignore` for that build. So this file decouples the two
# images:
#
#   docker build .                        -> uses .dockerignore
#   docker build -f Dockerfile.test .     -> uses this file
#
# Buildah — and therefore `podman build` — has no per-Dockerfile ignore file
# at all and falls back to `.dockerignore` for every build. Which means a
# machine may use either file for this image, and *neither* may exclude what
# `Dockerfile.test` copies. Both are written to the same rule and one test
# checks both (ADR-104).
#
# The rule: exclude nothing the image copies, rather than exclude broadly
# and re-include with `!`. Negation is in the spec, but the re-includes did
# not take effect on the Podman path and the build failed on a
# `COPY … README.md` for a file plainly in the checkout. There is now
# nothing left to negate.

# Version control and local virtualenvs.
.git
.venv
venv

# Build and tool caches. Coverage output especially: copying a previous
# run's .coverage into the image would skew a fresh report.
__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
htmlcov
coverage.xml
.coverage

# The real secrets. The suite is designed to pass with no token, so neither
# must be in this context: .env is the demo's, .env.test is the end-to-end
# tier's (ADR-075). The gate never reads either.
#
# `.env.example` is NOT excluded — it is test input, and Dockerfile.test
# copies it.
.env
.env.test
.env.test.example

# Prose neither image builds from. Directories, not `*.md`: that pattern
# also caught `README.md` (the port-agreement test, ADR-081), every
# `skills/**/SKILL.md` (ADR-038) and the forks' `NOTICE.md` (ADR-044).
docs

# Runtime state, never build input (ADR-035, ADR-065).
workspace
memory
agent_memory_db

# NOTE: `tests/`, `scripts/` and `observability/` are deliberately NOT
# listed — Dockerfile.test copies all three.
